WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.083
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.083 --> 00:00:06.972
For as long as I can remember,

00:00:06.972 --> 00:00:10.760
there have been two people inside my head
whispering to me.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:14.340
Now, I wanna introduce them to you, and

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.300
I think the best way to do that is with an
experience I had last year,

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.710
in probably my least favorite place in the
entire world.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:24.925
The customer service line at United
Airlines.

00:00:24.925 --> 00:00:25.740
[LAUGH].

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.270
Now I had gotten to SFO and

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.820
hour early to have plenty of time to make
my direct flight to JFK.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.990
I was looking forward to landing late that
evening and

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.980
getting a good night sleep before my
friends wedding the following afternoon.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:43.020
Instead, as I was going through security I
got a text from United saying

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.630
my flight was cancelled for airplane
maintenance.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.590
I rush to the customer service line where
after waiting my turn, the woman

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:55.510
behind the counter told me there was no
way I could get to New York before Sunday.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:56.950
My response?

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:00.230
Wait, you're telling me it'll take two
days to get from San Francisco to

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:00.950
New York?

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:02.890
That can't be right, please check again.

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:06.425
The woman did not take kindly to my tone.

00:01:06.425 --> 00:01:06.960
[LAUGH].

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.520
And pushed a few buttons before telling me
I could take a two layover trip, and

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.380
make the tail end of the reception, take
it or leave it.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:16.640
I tried after the fact to be friendly, but

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.930
couldn't get any other options and I took
it.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:22.420
Later I found out a friend behind me in
line had patiently managed

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.370
to get a one lay over trip and make the
ceremony.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:28.830
So that's what happened outside my head
out in the world, but

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.950
here's what happened with those two people
inside my head.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:36.800
Now the first person, imagine him
whispering to me from my left shoulder,

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.490
I call the eager updater.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.880
I got to the airport, saw the cancellation
text, and

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.048
the eager updater chimed right into my
head.

00:01:45.048 --> 00:01:50.020
He said, hm, you know, it looks like the
plane we

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.785
expected to take us to New York isn't
going there after all.

00:01:52.785 --> 00:01:54.540
[LAUGH].

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.620
Good to know, what's our best option now?

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:03.160
As I dealt with the customer service lady,
the eager updater immediately noticed,

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.648
she wasn't the instant ally I had been
counting on.

00:02:05.648 --> 00:02:06.409
[LAUGH].

00:02:06.409 --> 00:02:07.542
He said.

00:02:07.542 --> 00:02:08.458
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:02:08.458 --> 00:02:09.940
Good to know.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:12.660
Now we can adjust our approach to get the
best outcome

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.610
from the woman who is in front of us and
not the woman we thought she'd be.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:19.840
Pretty mature, right?

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:20.880
Kind of suspicious though.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.470
Like, if the eager updater was whispering
all of this useful stuff to me,

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:26.990
how come I acted so ineffectively.

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:29.642
Well, that's where the second person in my
head comes in.

00:02:29.642 --> 00:02:30.640
[LAUGH].

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.130
I call him the king of wishful thinking.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:35.710
Imagine him whispering to me from my right
shoulder.

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:39.960
Now, the king of wishful thinking saw the
cancellation text and

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.399
he too chimed right into my head.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.600
He said this shouldn't be happening.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:49.180
We were just about to be on our way to New
York and

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.520
now we have to deal with a frustrating day
of travel hassle.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.840
Every minute I stood in the customer
service line,

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:59.590
the king of wishful thinking was
whispering reminders to me.

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:02.375
You know we'd expected to be in the air by
now.

00:03:02.375 --> 00:03:03.230
[LAUGH].

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:07.900
By now, we'd be over Colorado on our way
to a nice warm hotel room.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.190
As I dealt with the customer service lady,
the king of wishful thinking said to me,

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:16.830
if someone competent was back there, we
would, they would have fixed this by now.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.305
This shouldn't be happening.

00:03:18.305 --> 00:03:18.824
[LAUGH].

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:24.720
So those are the two folks whispering in
my head all the time.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.480
The eager updater with his various forms
of, good to know.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.320
And the king of wishful thinking riffing
on his awesome catch-phrase,

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:32.690
this shouldn't be happening.

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:38.270
And if these guys are starting to sound
familiar to you, they should because

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:42.690
these two characters are whispering in
your head all the time too.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:45.350
The eager updater and the king of wishful
thinking are just

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:49.140
two ways our brains are wired to receive
and process information.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:54.850
Over the past couple of years, I've gotten
really interested in the neuroscience and

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:57.380
psychology behind how we handle
situations.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:01.170
And I've been studying in particular this
duality between perception and

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:01.950
expectation.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.570
I gave names and

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:08.910
personalities to these mental processes
really just to help me more easily apply

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.590
what I've learned to begin to make better
decisions in my own life.

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:15.480
And I wanna share them with you because I
think that getting

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.630
more acquainted with these guys in each of
our heads can help us handle,

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:23.770
be, be more effective in handling a wide
variety of situations we face every day.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:29.610
So I think of the eager updater as this
guy that openly seeks

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:34.150
out new information to update our view of
what is happening in any situation.

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:42.020
He plugs into our five senses to, to add
more data, so we can better understand,

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:45.880
accept and respond to the real world in
which we operate.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:49.500
The king of wishful thinking, on the other
hand,

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:53.600
filters every experience we have through
the lens of our expectations.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.370
Our hopes, our beliefs, our anticipations
about what will happen, or

00:04:57.370 --> 00:04:59.760
what should happen in any situation.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:05.230
Now maybe you're having visions right now
of old cartoons with an eager updater

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:08.460
angel on one shoulder and a king of
wishful thinking devil on the other.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.395
That's not fair to the king of wishful
thinking.

00:05:10.395 --> 00:05:12.370
[LAUGH].

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:16.880
We evolved this expectation lens for very
good reasons.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:19.560
For one being able to set a future goal
and

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:24.490
hold it in mind, keeps us motivated and
proactive as we work to achieve it.

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.670
It also keeps us mentally efficient.

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:33.150
Allowing us to focus on just the most
relevant parts of our surroundings.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:34.330
The neuroscience research for

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:39.320
example, suggests that our eyes actually
use our expectations to fill out most

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:43.380
of what we see with just small amounts of
new data informing the picture.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:45.270
The problem,

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:51.180
though, is that even though the king of
wishful thinking is useful and necessary,

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:56.620
he does have a tendency to get much louder
than our eager updater and drown him out.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:59.150
And that's what happened with my
experience with United Airlines.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:02.630
But this tendency applies well beyond
changing travel plans.

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:06.440
So for example, an over assertive king of
wishful thinking,

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:09.400
contributed to my staying in relationships
too long.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:13.810
When I clung to an image of the girl I
hoped I was dating, and filtered out

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:16.090
the information the real world that was
showing me something different.

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:19.660
Or beyond my own personal experience,

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:23.400
I was chatting about this topic last month
with an entrepreneur here in the Valley,

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:26.720
who immediately recognized the king of
wishful thinking in himself.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:29.860
As the number one reason his first startup
hadn't taken off.

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:31.640
For one thing,

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:37.060
he said, he had really wanted all of his
early employees to be high performers.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:40.460
And so, didn't acknowledge soon enough
that some of them were not and

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:41.719
act quickly to course correct.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:47.830
Similarly, he said he had expected that
the product offering he'd come up with

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:49.500
would be very difficult to replicate.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:53.090
And so it wasn't attentive to the
competitors that started to enter and

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:53.850
gain market share.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:59.960
Now, hopefully, each of these experiences
reinforces that our king of wishful

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:06.110
thinking can be a loud and damaging part
of our psychology and our neurophysiology.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:08.300
But that doesn't mean we just have to
throw up our hands.

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:12.830
The research suggests there are two
relatively simple steps we can take

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:14.660
to bring him more inline.

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:21.020
And the first step is simply to recognize
that we all have

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:24.570
a king of wishful thinking whispering
inside our heads all the time.

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:26.690
And be aware when he might be getting too
dominant.

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:32.110
Now here the signs suggest there are five
emotions that are the most useful red

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:33.460
flags for this.

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:35.736
And as I list them think about a time you
recently felt these.

00:07:35.736 --> 00:07:40.830
Frustration, anger, jealousy,

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:48.319
irritation, disappointment, recognize
those?

00:07:48.319 --> 00:07:49.670
[LAUGH].

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:52.355
I know I felt three or four of them before
breakfast.

00:07:52.355 --> 00:07:53.980
[LAUGH].

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:59.110
Well, it turns out these are signals of an
overaggressive king of wishful thinking,

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:02.990
basically telling us that there's
something that we were hoping for or

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:06.040
expecting from reality that isn't there.

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:08.600
Basically telling us this shouldn't be
happening.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:15.040
The second step then is, in those
situations, to summon a more attentive and

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:17.550
assertive eager updater.

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:22.980
And the main strategy here is to open out
our perceptual field, by asking questions

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:26.769
like, how might I respond differently now,
given the new information available?

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:31.410
Or by responding to the King's, this
shouldn't be happening,

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.965
with this is happening, good to know.

00:08:33.965 --> 00:08:36.650
[LAUGH].

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:42.420
With just these two steps, we can change
the way we experience the setbacks and

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:47.270
opportunities in our lives, with the
benefit of a more clear-sighted and

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:50.350
constructed view of what's going on around
us.

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:54.690
And that's my hope for myself, and it's my
hope for each of you,

00:08:54.690 --> 00:08:58.790
that next time you're working hard to
solve a complex problem, or

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:04.160
grow a promising company, or take the next
step in a relationship, or

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:06.695
heaven forbid, reschedule a flight on
United.

00:09:06.695 --> 00:09:08.043
[LAUGH].

00:09:08.043 --> 00:09:13.103
I hope you stop, take a mental step back,
and readjust the volume

00:09:13.103 --> 00:09:18.083
setting of the two companions whispering
from your shoulders.

00:09:18.083 --> 00:09:18.708
Thank you.

00:09:18.708 --> 00:09:26.125
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:26.125 --> 00:09:30.699
[MUSIC]

